    <h2>1. The NFPA 54 Longest Length Method</h2>
    <p>
      Plumbing codes (NFPA 54 / National Fuel Gas Code and International Fuel Gas Code) strictly prohibit sizing individual branches in isolation. You must use the <strong>Longest Length Method</strong>:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Measure the total linear distance from the gas utility meter (or 2-psi regulator) to the <em>farthest gas appliance</em> in the entire home. That distance becomes the design column for the entire system.</li>
      <li>If your furnace is 70 feet from the meter, every section of pipe in the house must be sized using the 70-foot or 80-foot table row, even if your tankless heater is only 20 feet away from the meter.</li>
      <li>Add the equivalent length for all 90-degree elbows and tees (a 3/4-inch 90-degree elbow adds 2.06 feet of frictional flow resistance).</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>2. Schedule 40 Black Steel Gas Pipe Capacity Table (0.5 psi / 0.5 in w.c. drop)</h2>
    <p>
      Capacities listed below represent thousands of BTUs/hr (MBH) for Natural Gas (0.60 Specific Gravity, 1,000 BTU/cu ft):
    </p>
