    <h2>3. Can You Ever Run a 199,000 BTU Tankless on a 1/2" Pipe?</h2>
    <p>
      Some modern tankless manufacturers (like Navien and Rinnai) market units with "1/2-inch gas line compatibility." Homeowners frequently misinterpret this claim:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The Strict Limitation:</strong> A 1/2-inch black iron pipe can ONLY deliver 199,000 BTUs if the total gas pipe length from the meter is <strong>less than 10 to 12 feet</strong>, AND the unit operates on a dedicated run with minimal elbows, AND the inlet dynamic gas pressure never drops below 5.0 inches of water column.</li>
      <li><strong>In Real World Retrofits:</strong> Water heaters are almost always located in basements or garages 25 to 60 feet away from the gas meter. At 40 feet, a 1/2-inch pipe delivers only 81,000 BTUs—less than half of what the tankless needs. Running on 1/2-inch pipe in these conditions will cause the unit to flameout during cold winter days when full burner modulation is required.</li>
      <li><strong>The Pro Rule:</strong> Always run <strong>3/4-inch</strong> (for runs under 40 feet) or <strong>1-inch</strong> (for runs over 40 feet).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. CSST Flexible Corrugated Stainless Tubing vs Black Iron</h2>
    <p>
      If running rigid black iron pipe through finished basement ceilings is difficult, CSST (Corrugated Stainless Steel Tubing, such as TracPipe or Gastite) offers a flexible alternative:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>CSST Friction Factor:</strong> Because of internal corrugated ridges, CSST has higher friction than smooth black iron pipe. A 3/4-inch CSST line carries significantly fewer BTUs than a 3/4-inch rigid iron pipe.</li>
      <li><strong>Upsizing Rule:</strong> When using CSST for a 199,000 BTU tankless water heater, you almost always need to upsize to <strong>1-inch CSST</strong> for runs over 20 feet.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- Trade Pro Field Note -->
  <div class="mb-10">
